### What each check tells you

| Check | What it verifies | What to do if it fails |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Lint | The configured code rules | Read the file and line in the output and fix the violation. |
| Security scan | Committed-secret checks in the supplied script | Remove exposed values from tracked files and rotate leaked secrets. The reference reports npm audit findings separately for review. |
| Jest | The assertions in your unit and component tests | Open the failing test and compare its expected and actual result. |
| E2E type check | TypeScript device-test files compile | Fix the reported types or missing imports. |
| Coverage | How much production code the tests execute | Open the report and add tests for uncovered behavior. |
| Device suite | The selected user journey works in the installed app | Inspect the failing assertion, device log, and available video. |

## Keep credentials out of committed files

The reference security scan checks ignored secret files and generated credentials. Keep `.env`, `.vault/secrets.env`, `.provision.env`, keystores, certificates, provisioning profiles, and populated `src/config/buildEnv.ts` out of commits. Commit only the empty build-config placeholder. Rotate exposed credentials before cleaning them from history.

Teams Workflows URLs contain a secret token. Treat the full URL as a credential. The reference's npm audit runs in report-only mode, with high and critical findings reviewed each release.

The reference exposes `TEST_PASSWORD` to merge-request builds and keeps Sauce keys out of them. Have the CI owner confirm that policy and its branch trust settings for your app. The guide's local and live verification status is recorded in the [complete reference](/mobile/cicd/mobile-test-automation-reference/).

## Read the reference scripts correctly

The supplied `scripts/lint.sh` skips lint if ESLint is absent. Check the log to confirm which checks actually ran. The reference's `scripts/security-scan.sh` checks tracked secret files and generated credentials, with npm audit findings reported for review. A green local command alone cannot establish that lint, coverage, or real-device assertions executed.

For the standalone reference, protect the default branch against direct pushes and force pushes and require the pipeline to pass before merge. The framework setup guide includes smoke tests for feature pushes, merge-request status, post-merge device execution, and rejected direct pushes. Follow [the setup verification instructions](/mobile/cicd/mobile-test-automation-reference/#8-verify-the-setup-smoke-tests) in an isolated approved exercise.

## Set the coverage target

**The team requirement is 100% for the defined production scope and supported metrics.** The supplied reference command list does not establish a coverage gate. The configuration and verifier below are documentation examples for adding that requirement to your existing setup.

Run Android commands from the Gradle root and iOS tests from the configured Xcode project. Run the example coverage verifier from the Git repository root.

## Understand what 100% means

For each supported metric, all measured code must be covered. The report must include every required executable production file, including files no test imported. Checking only changed files is insufficient.

A line executed without an assertion can still be covered. Add the order validation, confirmation, service-failure, and repeated-submission assertions from [Writing Tests](/mobile/testing-quality/writing-tests/); do not write empty tests simply to execute lines.

